Repairing the production fault — tracing the failure to its root cause.
I've pinpointed the mismatch error to absolute-form request handling — now examining the URL logic.
The captured request uses an absolute URI — now verifying how the framework parses it.
Local verification shows the invariant now holds — confirming against the full incident replay.
Root cause: for absolute-form request targets (`GET http://example.com/u?q=1`), `req.url`/`originalUrl` is a full URL. `request.href` already handled this (returns `originalUrl` directly), but `request.URL` unconditionally built `new URL(protocol://host + originalUrl)`, producing garbage (`http://127.0.0.1:47901http://example.com/...`), throwing, and falling back to `Object.create(null)`. The probe `ctx.URL.href !== ctx.href` then mismatched (`undefined` vs URL string) → HTTP 500 `E_URL_MISMATCH`. `parseurl`-based `path`/`query` already parsed absolute URLs correctly, so only `URL` was broken.

Files changed:
- `lib/request.js` (`get URL`): if `originalUrl` matches `/^https?:\/\//i`, parse it directly with `new URL(originalUrl)`; otherwise keep existing `protocol://host+originalUrl` construction. Mirrors the existing `href` absolute-URL handling.

Validation:
- Local check: `originalUrl=http://example.com/u?q=1` now gives `href == URL.href == http://example.com/u?q=1`, `path==/u`.
- `/work/repro`: before `ORIGINAL_FAILURE_REPRODUCED / 500`; after `ORIGINAL_FAILURE_ABSENT / 200`.
- `node --test` (full repo suite): 452 pass, 0 fail.

Remaining uncertainty: none for this incident shape; WHATWG normalization edge cases (e.g. explicit default ports, case) could still make `URL.href !== href` string-wise, but do not occur here.

